-- Query 1: Overall attrition rate-- Result: 16.12% left. This is the baseline for comparison.SELECT     Attrition,    COUNT(*) AS employee_count,    ROUND(COUNT(*) * 100.0 / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM employees), 2) AS percentageFROM employeesGROUP BY Attrition;-- Query 2: Attrition by department-- Result: Sales has the highest attrition at 20.63%, followed closely by HR at 19.05%--both above the company-wide average of 16.12%. R&D, by contrast, has notably lower attrition at--13.84%, suggesting something about that department (workload, management, career growth, compensation) --may be retaining employees better than Sales or HR. SELECT     Department,    COUNT(*) AS total_employees,    SUM(CASE WHEN Attrition = 'Yes' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS left_count,    ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN Attrition = 'Yes' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) * 100.0 / COUNT(*), 2) AS attrition_rateFROM employeesGROUP BY DepartmentORDER BY attrition_rate DESC;--Query 3 — Overtime vs attrition--Overtime is the strongest attrition driver found so far — employees who work overtime--leave at 30.53%, compared to just 10.44% for those who don't, nearly a 3x difference.--This far exceeds the gap seen between departments (Sales at 20.63% vs R&D at 13.84%), --suggesting overtime may be a more powerful predictor of attrition than department itself.SELECT     OverTime,    COUNT(*) AS total,    SUM(CASE WHEN Attrition = 'Yes' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS left_count,    ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN Attrition = 'Yes' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) * 100.0 / COUNT(*), 2) AS attrition_rateFROM employeesGROUP BY OverTime;-- Query 4 — Job role tenure before leaving-- Sales Representatives don't just have high attrition — they leave the fastest, averaging -- just 2.09 years of tenure before departing, the shortest of any role. Laboratory Technicians-- account for the most departures overall (62 people), even though their average tenure before-- leaving (3.16 years) is a bit longer. This suggests Sales Representative roles specifically -- may have an early-tenure retention problem — worth checking whether these are also the employees -- most affected by overtime.SELECT     JobRole,    ROUND(AVG(YearsAtCompany), 2) AS avg_years,    COUNT(*) AS total_who_leftFROM employeesWHERE Attrition = 'Yes'GROUP BY JobRoleORDER BY avg_years ASC;-- Query 5 — Income comparison-- Employees who left earned about $2,046 less per month on average than those who stayed-- ($4,787 vs $6,833) — a roughly 30% gap. Combined with the earlier findings, this paints-- a fairly coherent picture: employees most likely to leave tend to be in lower-paying,-- high-overtime roles like Sales Representative, and they leave early, often within 2-3 years.SELECT     Attrition,    ROUND(AVG(MonthlyIncome), 2) AS avg_incomeFROM employeesGROUP BY Attrition;